        "en" to GemDetails(
            "Marcasite is, like pyrite, an iron sulphide with the formula FeS₂, but crystallised in another system: it is a polymorph. It forms at lower temperature and in more acidic conditions than pyrite, mainly in near-surface sediments, chalks, clays and coals, where it precipitates from acidic solutions. It is found as nodules in the chalk of northern France and southern England, and as “cockscomb” twinned crystals. Unstable, it gradually turns into powdery sulphates in humid air.",
            "Orthorhombic system. Tabular crystals twinned into cockscombs or spearheads, radiating nodules and concretions. Poor cleavage; uneven fracture. Hardness 6 to 6.5; specific gravity 4.85 to 4.92, slightly lighter than pyrite; bronze-yellow metallic lustre paler than pyrite, turning greenish grey; opaque; greenish-black streak. “Pyrite disease”, which eats away at collections, affects it particularly. For this reason true marcasite is never used in jewellery: so-called “marcasite” jewellery is set with pyrite.",
            "France: the chalk cliffs of the Boulonnais and the Pays de Caux, with nodules gathered on the beaches. England: Dover and Folkestone. Czech Republic: Bohemia (Vintířov), famous for its cockscombs. Germany, United States (Missouri, Wisconsin, Illinois), Mexico and Peru. Its name is thought to come from Arabic or Persian and was long applied to both iron sulphides.",
            "A stone of clairvoyance and protection, said to strengthen willpower and dispel confusion. It is associated with the solar plexus chakra and the sign of Leo. Like pyrite, never cleanse it in water; keep it dry, ideally with a moisture absorber; cleanse by smudging."
